<section class="race-hero" aria-label="Racing">
  <video
    class="race-hero__video"
    autoplay
    muted
    loop
    playsinline
    poster="/images/racing/hero-poster.jpg?v=20260821e"
  >
    <source src="/videos/racing/hero.mp4?v=20260821e" type="video/mp4" />
  </video>
  <div class="race-hero__veil" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="race-hero__copy">
    <p class="race-hero__eyebrow">Racing</p>
    <h1 class="race-hero__title">Performance Starts With Physiology</h1>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel-white race-intro">
  <div class="race-intro__inner">
    <h2 class="race-intro__title">The Race Within the Race</h2>
    <p class="race-intro__body">
      Racing places extraordinary demands on the equine athlete. During intense exercise, the horse&rsquo;s demand
      for oxygen and energy can exceed its ability to maintain physiological balance. As the body shifts from
      efficient aerobic metabolism toward less efficient anaerobic energy production, fatigue accelerates,
      performance declines, recovery slows, and physiological stress begins to affect multiple interconnected
      body systems.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="race-cascade-board" id="industry-reality" aria-label="The Race Within the Race">
  <div class="race-cascade-board__frame">
    <img
      src="/images/racing/race-cascade-board.jpg?v=20260904a"
      sizes="100vw"
      alt="Phases of Performance Decline in an 8 Furlong Race, due to insufficient oxygen to the muscles. As oxygen demand exceeds supply, a physiological cascade is triggered, leading to fatigue, stress, and decline. Phase 1, Optimal Performance, 0 to 1 furlong: muscles receive adequate oxygen, energy production efficient, horse feels strong and powerful. Phase 2, Oxygen Demand Exceeds Supply, 1 to 2 furlongs: oxygen demand begins to outpace supply as pace increases. Phase 3, Lactic Acid Accumulates, 2 to 4 furlongs: anaerobic metabolism increases, lactic acid builds up in muscle tissues. Phase 4, Cardiovascular Stress Increases, 4 to 5.5 furlongs: heart rate and blood lactate rise, cardiovascular system works harder to deliver limited oxygen. Phase 5, Performance Begins to Decline, 5.5 to 6.5 furlongs: fatigue sets in, stride length shortens, power and speed begin to fall. Phase 6, Pulmonary Stress Develops, 6.5 to 7.5 furlongs: breathing becomes labored, oxygen exchange efficiency declines. Phase 7, Recovery Slows and Damage Accumulates, 7.5 to 8 furlongs (finish): severe fatigue, recovery is slow, long-term structural damage accumulates with repeated stress. The cascade in summary: oxygen demand exceeds supply, lactic acid accumulates, cardiovascular stress increases, performance declines, pulmonary stress develops, recovery slows, long-term structural damage accumulates."
      class="race-cascade-board__img"
      width="1536"
      height="1024"
    />
  </div>
  <h2 class="sr-only">Phases of Performance Decline in an 8 Furlong Race</h2>
</section>

<section class="panel-white race-intro">
  <div class="race-intro__inner">
    <h2 class="race-intro__title">When Performance Declines, the Costs Begin</h2>
    <p class="race-intro__body">
      The physiological cascade doesn&rsquo;t end at the finish line. Its consequences extend far beyond race
      day&mdash;impacting soundness, longevity, veterinary costs, and the financial success of every horse owner
      and trainer.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

